    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A floating deck, also known as a freestanding deck, is an outdoor platform that sits on the ground or just above it, rather than being fixed to the house or set on deep concrete footings like a traditional deck. Because it needs no dug foundations and no ledger board bolted to the wall, it has become a favourite with DIYers and homeowners who want to transform an outdoor space quickly. Like any home improvement project, though, it comes with trade-offs you should weigh before you start.</p>

<h2 id="simple-installation">1. Simple installation</h2>
<p>The biggest advantage of a floating deck is how easy it is to build. A traditional deck usually means digging holes below the frost line, pouring concrete footings and carefully fixing the frame to the house with a ledger board. A floating deck skips those steps. Using pre-cast deck blocks or adjustable pedestals, you build the whole frame on top of prepared, level ground. With no rigid connection to the house, the deck can move slightly with the ground without pulling at the wall. That makes it a realistic project for anyone with basic carpentry skills, and a small deck can often be finished over a weekend or two.</p>

<h2 id="regulatory-ease">2. Fewer permissions (usually)</h2>
<p>Because a floating deck is not attached to the house and sits low to the ground, it often falls outside the rules that apply to raised decks. In England, for example, decking is generally allowed under permitted development if it is no more than 30 cm (12 in) above the ground and, together with other outbuildings, covers no more than half the garden. Many US building codes exempt small freestanding decks under 76 cm (30 in) high. Rules vary widely, though, and can be stricter for listed buildings, conservation areas, flats and homes near boundaries. Some councils and homeowners' associations also have rules on how close a structure can sit to a boundary, so always check with your local planning or building authority before you build.</p>

<h2 id="lower-cost">3. Lower cost</h2>
<p>A floating deck is usually one of the cheaper ways to add a deck. With no ledger board, no flashing against the wall and no deep concrete footings, the materials list is shorter. The work is also less technical, so many homeowners build it themselves, and if you do hire a builder, the job generally takes noticeably less labour than a raised, attached deck. The boards you choose still make the biggest difference to the final price; our <a href="/blog/wood-deck-cost">wood deck cost guide</a> explains what drives it.</p>

<h2 id="protects-the-house">4. Protects the house</h2>
<p>One less obvious benefit is what a floating deck does not do to your house. Attaching a deck normally means cutting into the cladding or render, bolting a ledger board to the structure and flashing the joint to keep water out. If that detail is done badly, water can get into the wall behind it and cause damp and rot that is difficult to put right.</p>
<p>A freestanding deck leaves the walls untouched, so there is no ledger joint to leak. That makes it a sensible choice for rendered or stone-faced homes and older properties where you do not want to drill into the fabric of the building. If your home is listed or in a conservation area, check the rules anyway, as even freestanding structures in the garden can need consent.</p>

<h2 id="versatility">5. Versatile placement</h2>
<p>"Floating" describes placement as well as structure. Because it is not tied to the house, a floating deck can go anywhere on the plot: a destination deck at the far end of the garden, a sunny corner, or a level platform beside an above-ground pool. It is a good way to reclaim an awkward or muddy corner by levelling it with gravel and turning it into a dining spot, a yoga platform or somewhere to read. Its low, simple shape also suits modern gardens, and you can use several small platforms to create separate zones for cooking, lounging and socialising. For layout ideas, see our <a href="/blog/floating-deck-ideas-small-yards">floating deck ideas for small yards</a>.</p>

<!--figure:timber-deck-in-woodland-garden|Because it is freestanding, a floating deck can go wherever the garden works best.-->

<h2 id="low-height-safety">6. Low height, lower fall risk</h2>
<p>Most floating decks are only a step or two above the ground, so the risk of a serious fall is much lower than on a raised deck, which is reassuring with young children or older relatives. Many are built without railings, which keeps the view open and lets the deck run straight onto the lawn. Keep edges and steps clearly visible, with a contrasting edge board or low lighting, so nobody trips at night.</p>

<!--figure:low-deck-with-rail-garden|Kept low to the ground, a floating deck carries less fall risk and often needs no railing.-->

<h2 id="easy-repairs">7. Easy repairs</h2>
<p>If part of a floating deck starts to dip as the ground settles, the fix is usually simple: lift that corner, re-level or pack the block, and lower it back down. Adjustable pedestals make this even easier. Compare that with repairing a sunken footing on a raised deck, which is a much bigger job.</p>

<h2 id="rot-risk">8. Rot risk and drainage</h2>
<p>The main drawback of a floating deck is how close it sits to the ground. With little space underneath, airflow is limited and moisture can get trapped, which leads to early rot, mould and mildew if the site is not prepared properly. To reduce the risk:</p>
<ul>
<li>Use pressure-treated timber rated for ground contact for the frame.</li>
<li>Make sure the area drains well; a very slight fall away from the house helps water run off.</li>
<li>Dig out a little topsoil, lay a weed membrane and add a layer of gravel, about 5 cm (2 in), before you assemble the frame.</li>
<li>Leave small gaps between deck boards, about 3–6 mm (1/8–1/4 in) or as the board manufacturer advises, and keep the sides partly open so air can circulate.</li>
</ul>

<!--figure:weathered-dark-deck-boards|Boards close to the ground dry out slowly, so drainage and airflow underneath matter.-->

<h2 id="terrain-sensitivity">9. Terrain sensitivity</h2>
<p>Floating decks are best on flat, level ground. If your garden has a noticeable slope, a floating deck is much harder to keep stable. A traditional deck uses posts of different heights to cope with uneven ground, whereas a floating deck relies on the surface beneath it. Adjustable pedestals or a little excavation can correct small dips, but a steep slope will almost always need a properly anchored deck designed for the site.</p>

<h2 id="longevity-concerns">10. Longevity concerns</h2>
<p>Because it is exposed to more ground moisture and has no deep foundation, a floating deck may not last as long as a well-built attached deck. How long yours lasts depends on the materials, your climate, drainage and maintenance. In colder climates it can also be affected by frost heave, where freezing ground expands and tilts or shifts the deck. This is usually easy to fix by re-levelling, but it is a job that owners of deeper-founded decks rarely face. Composite boards resist rot better than most timber, though the frame underneath still needs to be durable.</p>

<h2 id="maintenance">11. More cleaning</h2>
<p>Low decks are more exposed to splash-back, where rain hits the ground and throws soil and moisture onto the edge boards and frame. That means a floating deck may need cleaning more often than a raised one. Sweep off leaves and debris regularly, keep gravel or stone borders clear so water drains away, and clean and re-treat timber when the finish starts to wear, following the product's instructions. If you use a pressure washer, use a low setting and a fan nozzle, and follow the decking manufacturer's guidance.</p>

<h2 id="who-is-it-right-for">12. Who is it right for?</h2>
<p>A floating deck is a good solution for:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>DIYers on a budget:</strong> it usually costs less than a raised deck because it needs fewer materials and no concrete work.</li>
<li><strong>Renters:</strong> a small, modular floating deck can sometimes be taken apart when you move. Get your landlord's written permission first, as changes to the garden may still be covered by your tenancy agreement.</li>
<li><strong>Separate zones:</strong> if you want a quiet seating area in a far corner of the garden, away from the house, a freestanding deck is often the simplest way to create it.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Before you buy materials, mark out the deck's footprint with string and pegs and leave it for a few days. Put your outdoor chairs or table inside it to check the size works and that you still have room to walk around.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
<h3>Does a floating deck need a railing?</h3>
<p>In many places a low deck does not legally need a railing; many US codes only require guards above 76 cm (30 in). Check your local rules, and consider a railing or built-in edge seating anyway if young children or older relatives will use the deck.</p>
<h3>What is the best wood for a floating deck?</h3>
<p>Pressure-treated timber rated for ground contact is essential for the frame, because it sits so close to the ground. For the deck boards, naturally durable woods such as cedar, or composite decking, are good choices because they resist rot and insects.</p>
<h3>Do I need to remove the grass under a floating deck?</h3>
<p>Yes, it is strongly recommended. Strip the turf, lay a weed membrane and cover it with gravel, about 5 cm (2 in). This stops weeds growing up between the boards and improves drainage under the deck.</p>
<h3>Can I put a hot tub on a floating deck?</h3>
<p>Generally, no. Floating decks are designed for people and light furniture. A filled hot tub is extremely heavy and needs a reinforced concrete base or a structure engineered for the load, so follow the manufacturer's installation guidance.</p>
<h3>Can I build a floating deck over an existing concrete patio?</h3>
<p>Yes, and it is a popular way to cover an old, cracked patio. Use adjustable deck pedestals or low sleeper joists to level the new surface and keep the timber lifted clear of the concrete, so rainwater can still drain away underneath.</p>
<h3>Will a floating deck blow away in high winds?</h3>
<p>It is unlikely in most gardens, because the weight of the frame, boards and furniture holds it down. On very exposed sites, or for a small, light platform, ask a builder whether ground anchors are worth adding, and secure tall screens or parasols separately.</p>
<h3>How long does a floating deck last?</h3>
<p>Lifespan depends on the timber, your climate, drainage and how well you maintain it. With good site preparation, quality materials and regular cleaning and treating, a floating deck can last many years, and composite boards can extend that further.</p>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<p>A floating deck is one of the quickest ways to turn a patch of lawn into somewhere you actually want to sit, as long as your ground is level and you prepare the site for drainage. For styling ideas once it is built, read our <a href="/blog/terrace-and-garden-lounge-styling-guide">terrace and garden lounge styling guide</a> and <a href="/blog/small-balcony-decorating-ideas">small balcony decorating ideas</a>, or explore our <a href="/services/balcony-terrace-decoration">balcony and terrace decoration</a> service. To plan your outdoor space with a designer, <a href="/book-consultation">book a consultation</a>.</p>
